--TEST--
A capability nobody granted throws a `RuntimeError` naming the member, the capability in its configuration spelling and the argument that fell outside the grant, over a `help:` line, and the statement after the `catch` runs — `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/capability-denials-are-catchable
// `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error` makes a denial an ordinary throw
// rather than an escalation, so the arm real code already carries is the arm
// that has to take it: `RuntimeError`, and not the `Throwable` a program only
// writes when it means to catch everything.

try {
    Core\IO::read("orders.csv");
    echo "unreachable: the read returned\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo $denied->message, "\n";
}

// `fs.read` and `fs.write` are separate grants, so the message names the one
// that was missing rather than the namespace holding both — the reader of it is
// the operator, and what they need is the string they will write down.
try {
    Core\IO::write("orders.csv", "id,total");
    echo "unreachable: the write returned\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A denial is known before any work is done and leaves nothing behind, so the
// program is where it was: the next statement is the one after the `catch`.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for orders.csv, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
Core\IO::write needs the capability `fs.write` for orders.csv, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
still running
